ss and therefore  characterization of systemic amyloid  deposits by mass spectrometry is already  in clinically implemented in several  hospitals and reference laboratories  some of the biomarkers are currently in  research include on the breast cancer  marker her2 and the immune checkpoint  PDL one which was actually presented at  one of the poster session which is a  collaboration between MD Anderson and  University of Chicago  so this is the end of my presentation  but I just wanted to quickly summarize  assemble two take-home messages from my  talk and I hope that the audience can  appreciate how far we have come in terms  of using this technology that's been  around for decades and how we are able  to advance medical diagnosis with its  implementation in clinical laboratories  now it is certainly not it does not come  without listen limitation there is this  upfront cost that we talked about how it  can be a big capital for some  institution and it also involves  personnel training and method  development and establishing a  sustainable workflow but I really  believe with increased demand and  practices calling for Standardization  there will be a push for an approval of  an automated system in the US now it  might take some time to be able to  formulate kit for each SI but I think  there is certainly a market for it so  with that I would like to thank the  audience for listening in and  participating in this webinar and I am  open for our live questions  dr. chan for your informative  presentation we will now start the live  Q&amp;A portion of our webinar now we  already have some great questions coming  in but as a reminder to our audience if  you do have a question you would like to  ask dr. chan just click on that ask a  question box located on the far left of  your screen and we will answer as many  of your questions as we have time for ok  let's get started  dr. chan let's go with this first  question what resources are needed to  establish a clinical mass spectometry  laboratory hi that's a really good  question so one would like would you  know start with the capital requirement  for purchasing an acquaintance equipment  so as I mentioned earlier it usually  caused about 200,000 to 500,000  depending on the resolution of the mass  spec that you're looking at and of  course the lab space one would make sure  that there is enough lab space to put  this equipment is a pretty big equipment  and the other one is clinical need  assessment is there a clinical need for  example I talked a little bit about some  of the immunosuppressants that can be  used to measure the using mass  spectrometry and with their need do you  guys have do we have a big transplant  program what is the volume to bring in  specific tests if we're sending out some  esoteric tests do we have an increased  volume that it could justify the cost to  bring it in-house so there are some of  things to consider and certainly not not  to forget training of qualified  personnel to perform high complexity  testing is important it does take some  time to train a personnel to be  competent at performing this very high  complexity testing in my personal  experience it sometimes takes four to  six months to train somebody without any  experience so certainly consider all  this points to establish a successful  mass spectrometry lab  thank you dr. sham our next question  since most MS assays are lab developed  tests how do you maintain an insurer  test quality  yeah so it is important to establish  performance monitoring program by  tracking daily data analysis such as you  know running to see monitoring the  internal standard looking at the  retention time making sure that what  you're measuring eludes out at the same  time all the time  so that we know that it's consistent is  the same and a lot you're looking at we  want to look at the peak shape making  sure you track the pressure and then the  most important thing is establish an  acceptable trash hole what is considered  as that acceptable now the you know the  lab director will have to establish this  because this is a lab develop test  there's no necessary guidance from any  regulatory body it is also important to  catalog and track Lots when you open a  new lot of certainly agents and make  sure that you put in our expiration date  and perform a lot to Lock comparison in  patients every time you change a lot you  make sure that you perform lots of uh  comparison making sure that the same  patient still measures the same despite  in changing the lot and it is also  important to perform internal audit  program or external audit programs such  as subscribing to cap peat proficiency  testing or alternate alternative  assessment program when one cannot  subscribe to PT because they don't have  it so you would want to exchange  specimens with other laboratory  performing the same test making sure  that you establish an acceptable number  or you know limit of error of what  you're looking at  thank you for MS analysis on proteins do  you pretreat samples to increase  sensitivity and specificity and if yes  how do you do that yeah this is a really  good question  so for protein analysis especially in  matrix such as the whole blood or serum  it is a good idea to pretreat the  proteins so for those who are not  familiar with this pre treating means  that if you have a blood or urine or you  know some specimen that is that consists  of many different kinds of proteins in  there how do you purify them to increase  sensitivity and specificity so it is  very common for one when they develop  this test to use a monoclonal antibody  to bind or sometimes people call it a  fish forward and which for a particular  protein of interest that you're looking  for so therefore once you have a  monoclonal antibody that binds to the  protein of interest it would precipitate  so therefore you can increase the  selectivity sensitivity of your si  another thing is to perform our albumin  depletion because most of most of the  specimens consist of high percentage of  albumin so if you can pre treat it with  albumin depletion treatment you can get  rid of abundant non targeted proteins  and therefore you can increase  sensitivity and specificity of your  assay  thank you now we have time for just a  few more questions but I want to remind  our audience that those questions we are  unable to answer today and those that  come in during the on demand period will  be answered by our speaker via the email  address you provided at the time of  registration dr. Tam  what are some of the limitations of the  multitask in the identification of  microorganism yeah so again the the top  limitation is of course the initial high  cost of the instrument but the  technology itself in terms of limitation  is the identification of new isolates as  possible only if the spectral database  contains peptide mask fingerprints of  the type strains of specific species or  subspecies so if the library or your  database doesn't have that you cannot  match it once it produces or generates a  perspective then you can't really  release the result because they don't  it's not in the database but however may  and I do know for sure that Mayo Clinic  has generated their own database where  they collect different strain and  species of microorganism and start  building their own database that they  commonly see therefore they have  actually a large library compared to the  standard ones that we get in the lab  from the vendor  now our last question you mentioned mass  spectometry replacing gel  electrophoresis how does one bill for  the test does it have a CPT code it's  actually a very excellent question so  that I am myself questioned that a whole  so so currently there is no CPT code to  order the mass fix SI which is the mass  spectrometry method of analyzing the  monoclonal antibodies for monoclonal  gammopathy I do know that Mayo Clinic  currently offered that to the patients  who are currently seen at the Mayo  Clinic in terms of how they get charged  I'm not so sure we will have to ask them  and I do know that if you were to order  it from a reference standpoint say you  know I don't have this test I want to  order it from Mayo how do we get bill  for that that I do not know I've never  send them send it to them before but I  do know is that they have submitted an  application to CMS to apply for a CPT  code so it would be interesting to see  by next year so if there would be a code  for them and you know and we would know  how to charge our patients because I  think that it will become more of a  standard practice to use that technology  in terms of measuring monoclonal  gammopathy instead of gel  electrophoresis  Thank You dr. chan do you have any final  comments for our audience  no is that I'm very excited to share  this discussion with all of you and and  I enjoy presenting this presentation  thank you for the opportunity lab roots  thank you and I'd also like to thank our  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions one last  reminder that those questions we were  not able to answer to today and those  submitted during the on demand period  will be addressed by dr. chan via the  email address you provided at the time  of registration I would like to once  again thank dr. Amy Chan for her time  today and her important research would  also like to thank lab works for  bringing you today's live webinar this  webcast can be viewed on-demand and  laboratory alert you via email when it's  available from recall we encourage you  to share that email with your colleagues  who may have missed today's live event  that's all for now and we thank you for  joining us have a great day
 

